WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
When one version of a gene grows common, did selection favour it, or did plain luck?

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.520
Population-genetic models first predict what chance alone would do.

3
00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:19.453
At one locus, a place on a chromosome, a population carries gene versions called

4
00:00:19.453 --> 00:00:25.113
alleles. Two alleles, big A and little a, have allele frequencies p and q.

5
00:00:26.453 --> 00:00:28.393
Together, p + q = 1.

6
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:35.317
For Hardy-Weinberg proportions, suppose a large population mates randomly, with no

7
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:37.737
selection, mutation, or migration.

8
00:00:39.297 --> 00:00:44.277
Eggs and sperm pair by chance, so multiply their frequencies for each genotype, or

9
00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:50.057
allele pair. AA is p × p; aa is q × q.

10
00:00:51.057 --> 00:00:55.197
Aa forms both ways: p × q + q × p.

11
00:00:55.197 --> 00:00:56.197
For Hardy -Weinberg proportions,

12
00:00:56.217 --> 00:01:01.237
Recount big A. AA is all A: p × p.

13
00:01:02.297 --> 00:01:07.357
The two Aa tiles total 2pq; half their copies are A, giving pq.

14
00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:10.557
That sums to p × (p + q).

15
00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:14.497
Since p + q = 1, that stays p.

16
00:01:15.997 --> 00:01:18.997
Mating reshuffles alleles without changing their frequency.

17
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.740
In a small population, chance sampling moves allele frequencies: that is genetic

18
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:32.520
drift. Two chance steps drive it: which parental copy enters each egg or sperm, and

19
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.440
how many offspring each parent leaves.

20
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.720
Drift needs no difference in fitness, the relative number of surviving offspring.

21
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.480
So harmful alleles can rise, or beneficial ones vanish.

22
00:01:46.683 --> 00:01:51.263
Now start replicate populations at the same frequency and keep them alike.

23
00:01:52.623 --> 00:01:54.683
Let only chance act for many generations.

24
00:01:56.023 --> 00:01:59.323
What happens to the spread among replicates, and to their average?

25
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:09.747
Across replicates, drift spreads allele frequencies apart without pushing their

26
00:02:09.747 --> 00:02:15.227
average. Chance favours no allele, so the expected average stays put.

27
00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:19.447
That is the neutral null: what chance alone predicts.

28
00:02:20.927 --> 00:02:24.827
Consistent selection or gene flow from immigrants pushes the average.

29
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:31.830
One trap: a single population's frequency cannot tell luck from cause, since drift

30
00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.510
and selection both move it.

31
00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:37.650
So compare the replicates' average with the neutral null.

32
00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.590
Test selection with a fitness assay, and gene flow with ancestry.

33
00:02:43.833 --> 00:02:47.653
In conservation, genes can help a small population too.

34
00:02:48.973 --> 00:02:55.093
In 1995, managers released eight female pumas from Texas into southern Florida's

35
00:02:55.093 --> 00:02:57.673
small, inbred panther population.

36
00:02:59.013 --> 00:03:05.073
By 2010, panther numbers had about tripled, and heterozygosity, a measure of genetic

37
00:03:05.073 --> 00:03:06.433
variation, had doubled.

38
00:03:07.533 --> 00:03:13.753
In 2010, Hugo Snippert, Clevers and colleagues asked: does each division make one

39
00:03:13.753 --> 00:03:16.213
stem cell and one short-lived progenitor?

40
00:03:17.413 --> 00:03:20.953
They gave mouse small-intestinal stem cells one of four colours.

41
00:03:22.333 --> 00:03:23.933
Most divisions were symmetric.

42
00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:30.193
Over 1 to 6 months, some colour patches, each one cell's descendants, grew while

43
00:03:30.193 --> 00:03:36.493
others vanished. That fits stem-cell positions won or lost by chance: neutral drift.
